{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788979879,"nonce":"ZhsuNsLykDVQXE8X9vBE5C62cPE=","message":"Fixed in 2a0e4f1.\n\nThe audit found one live out-of-bounds read, not the several this described: the opcode interpreter's Opcode::Int did bytecode.ints[ptr.0]. Cranelift's main path already went through Function::int_at; only its division peephole (int_const_of) still read the pool directly, where a minted index answered None and silently skipped the optimization rather than reading out of bounds. The LLVM tier is safe by construction -- it materializes pending_ints as globals before lowering asks for one -- and the SSA walker was already correct.\n\nRather than a process-wide pool, the cached body now carries the ints minted for it (Body::Ready gained a second field) and Cache::int_at resolves the module pool first, then that list. This keeps the read on the interpreter's path a bounds check plus a slice index, which the perf-risk note asked for, and needs no global state.\n\nStill latent: no program in the corpus widens, and widening is the only pass that mints today, so the path is a guard rather than something exercised. The air-level contract it depends on is already pinned by the widen tests.","files":null}]}